.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt VIRTIO-NET 8
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtio-net
.Nd virtio network driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /sbin/virtio-net
.Op Ar bar Ns = Ns Ar n
.Op Ar bar_size Ns = Ns Ar bytes
.Op Ar dma Ns = Ns Ar bytes
.Op Ar irq Ns = Ns Ar line
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the userspace driver for a modern (virtio 1.0) PCI network function.  It is
launched by
.Xr devd 8
for a function whose vendor and device ids match the driver table, and it
receives four handles in the documented order: the device BAR (3), the
function's configuration page (4), a DMA buffer (5) and a legacy interrupt (6).
The command-line arguments are the values
.Nm
appends to the driver's arguments:
.Va bar ,
.Va bar_size ,
.Va dma
and
.Va irq .
.Pp
The driver resets the function, acknowledges it, negotiates
.Dv VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1
plus the MAC, MTU and link-status features, and programs split RX and TX
virtqueues in the DMA buffer.  Descriptors point directly into that buffer, so
frames are not copied between queues, and every used-ring index and length is
checked against the queue and buffer bounds before it is dereferenced.  The
device is interrupted through the transferred IRQ handle; the ISR byte and the
used rings are drained on each interrupt.  Received ARP requests directed at the
driver's MAC address are answered from the frame ring.
.Pp
When the driver exits it resets the function so the next launch starts from a
clean device.  The DMA buffer and the interrupt line belong to
.Xr devd 8 ,
which closes its own copies after the child exits; a crash or restart
therefore cannot leak either resource.
.Pp
The device must be a modern-only function (for example QEMU's
.Ql virtio-net-pci,disable-legacy=on ) whose registers live in a memory BAR.
Legacy virtio, MSI and MSI-X are not supported yet; the driver uses the
function's legacy INTx line.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/drivers.toml -compact
.It Pa /etc/drivers.toml
the driver table that launches
.Nm
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr devd 8 ,
.Xr pcid 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in RavenBSD 0.1.
.Sh STANDARDS
Virtio 1.0 (OASIS), PCI Local Bus 3.0.
